// Boot-gate UEFI sentinel, arm64 (sub-project D, L8). Booted as
// EFI/BOOT/BOOTAA64.EFI on an ESP handed to AAVMF, so the observable arrives
// on the SAME PL011 the firmware is already printing to.
//
// TWO MARKERS, AND THE ORDER BETWEEN THEM IS THE POINT — this is not a copy of
// sentinel_a64.kr with a different constant. The verdict function has to be
// able to tell "printed, then faulted" from "ran" and from "faulted", and a
// sentinel with ONE observable cannot produce the middle shape:
//
//   KRUEFI-A64   a LITERAL, printed first. It says the image loaded, the
//                firmware transferred control, and our code reached its first
//                statement. It proves nothing about arithmetic.
//   4000000016   a COMPUTED value: a static's initialiser mutated through a
//                call. The digit string appears NOWHERE in the artifact (the
//                static holds 4000000007), so a loader echo or an unexecuted
//                rodata blob cannot fake it.
//
// THE STATIC WRITE IS LAST, DELIBERATELY, and it is the fault site L8's
// printed-then-faulted control uses. With the section's write bit cleared
// (0x60000020) both markers reach the wire and the store then aborts — which
// is exactly the shape a verdict function that tests RAN first misfiles as
// success. Move `usink = v` above the prints and that control degrades into an
// ordinary "faulted without printing", and the six-outcome ordering in
// boot_gate.sh's uefi_verdict stops being exercised by anything.
//
// It is read back before returning so the store cannot be optimised away, and
// main returns 0: a non-zero return makes BDS treat the boot option as failed
// and fall through to the next one, which changes the firmware's own trailing
// output (measured: `return usink` => Boot0002 "EFI Internal Shell" instead of
// Boot0000 "UiApp"). The verdict never reads that, but the DONE marker does.
import "../std/uart_pl011.kr"
static uint64 useed = 4000000007
static uint64 usink = 0
fn ustamp(uint64 v) -> uint64 { return v + 9 }
fn main() -> uint64 {
    pl011_init()
    println_str("KRUEFI-A64")
    uint64 v = ustamp(useed)
    print(v)
    println_str("")
    usink = v
    if usink != v { return 1 }
    return 0
}
